PsyZ patch for the port. Written by this project; applies to PsyZ commit
4c68cab400cba341be3d2066993fcdf778301acf (port/external/psyz at its pin).
Applied by port/tools/psyzbuild.py to a copy of PsyZ, never to the submodule.

License: the PsyZ files this patch changes are under the
Mozilla Public License 2.0 by PsyZ's LICENSE file at that commit. The context
lines that the hunks below quote stay under that license, and this
change to them is offered under the same one: this Source Code Form is
subject to the terms of the Mozilla Public License, v. 2.0. A copy of it is
at https://mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/.

What it fixes: in GPU_Enqueue (psyz/src/psyz/libgpu.c), the path for hosts
whose unsigned long is 4 bytes draws the packets into PsyZ's vertex batch
but flushes the batch only when compiled for the browser (__EMSCRIPTEN__).
On a 32-bit native build nothing that a program draws reaches the picture
until something else flushes, and the readback of a drawn primitive shows
the clear colour only. In one run on one machine on 2026-10-09, PsyZ's own
picture tests built for i686 failed 25 of 37 in the gpu area and passed 37
of 37 with the flush made unconditional; no command of this repository
repeats that run. The 64-bit path does flush (through Psyz_GpuExeque), so
only the 32-bit path needs this.

At PsyZ commit 6d01339aadf16c5c9d6e44f44c0d66514752e472 the function
Psyz_GpuExeque also flushes the batch on 32-bit targets (PsyZ's change #178),
which covers the drop at the next DrawSync, LoadImage or StoreImage. On
2026-10-10 the port's picture test passed with this patch and without it, so
it does not show the patch to be needed there; the patch is kept.


Batched uploads: with Psyz_Sfa2SetBatchedUploads(1) (the port's setting fast_loads) the LoadImage calls that go into one command buffer share the
upload transfer buffer, one after the other, instead of cycling it for every call. A screen change makes the game call LoadImage over a thousand
times; each cycle made the driver allocate a new buffer of the whole size, and those calls took 160 to 600 ms. With it off nothing changes. The OpenGL backend (sdl3_gl.c) has a no-op of the same name, as for the other setters of the port, so that it links.

Output volume: Psyz_Sfa2SetVolume(gain) sets the gain of the one SDL audio stream that PsyZ opens (SDL_SetAudioStreamGain), and
Psyz_AudioInit applies the stored gain before it starts the stream, so a call before the device opens also counts. The port's
--volume PERCENT calls it. Nothing else of the audio path changes.

Optional graphics: the SDL_GPU backend consumes an explicit port marker for
super/life pixel-grid tiles and selected six-row health fills. It also exposes
an opt-in 384x224/4:3 canvas. With
both flags disabled, normal texture sampling and presentation remain unchanged.
The project-authored shared policy header is copied by psyzbuild.py. No shader
or global UV correction is changed. The port selects only its known HUD tiles.

Picture filter: Psyz_Sfa2SetFilter(filter) (the port's setting [display] filter) chooses how the SDL_GPU backend stretches the finished picture to its
place in the window: 0 nearest, 1 bilinear, 2 sharp bilinear. Nearest is the one nearest blit, as before. Bilinear is one blit with linear sampling, with no
enlargement first. Sharp bilinear first enlarges the picture by whole factors with nearest sampling, into a texture of its own, and stretches that texture
to its place in the window with linear sampling. The factors come from the shared policy header. When the window is a whole multiple of the picture on
both sides, sharp bilinear is the one nearest blit as before. The VRAM view of the debugger is never filtered. The OpenGL backend has a no-op of the same
name. This replaces Psyz_Sfa2SetSharpBilinear (port_video_api 3), which had only the choice of 0 and 2.

Screen: Psyz_Sfa2SetScreen(screen, w, h) puts the window in one of three states: 0 a window of the inner size w x h pixels, 1 exclusive fullscreen at the
mode of the window's display closest to w x h (0 x 0 is the desktop's size), 2 borderless fullscreen (no mode set, the desktop's size). Before the window
is shown the request is only kept, and the call returns 0; the window then gets the kept window size where PsyZ used its DEFAULT_FRONT_W x DEFAULT_FRONT_H
(1280 x 960) and the fullscreen is entered as the window is shown. After that the request is applied at once, and leaving fullscreen for the window gives
it the size of the call. SDL refusing a step makes the call return -1 and leaves the screen as it was; a fullscreen refused as the window is shown leaves a
window. PsyZ's own is_fullscreen flag follows every change, so its F4 key still works from any state. One line is printed for each change applied.
Psyz_Sfa2GetScreen returns the state in effect. Psyz_Sfa2DesktopSize gives the desktop mode's size of the window's display (-1 while there is no window),
and Psyz_Sfa2FullscreenModes the sizes of its fullscreen modes as w, h pairs, each once, largest first, at most as many as the caller has room for. The
OpenGL backend has no-ops (SetScreen and DesktopSize return -1, GetScreen and FullscreenModes 0).

Host keys: with Psyz_Sfa2SetHostKeys(1) the event pump no longer ends the program on Escape or toggles fullscreen on F4. A key press (not a repeat) of F1,
Escape or F4, and a gamepad's guide button, are only recorded, as the bits 1, 2, 4 and 8 of a mask that Psyz_Sfa2TakeKeys returns and clears. F6, the VRAM
view, and the closing of the window stay PsyZ's. With 0, the default, the keys are PsyZ's own and nothing is recorded. The OpenGL backend has no-ops.

